/volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#blues-rock","chapters":["Sección 3.2"],"pages":[[1,45,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":19,"text":"…Jones, Keith Richard, Eric Burdon, Jack Bruce, Ginger Baker, and spawned the similar combos of Cyril Davies, Graham Bond, Long John Baldry, and John Mayall. In 1962 a Seattle guitarist, Jimi Hendrix, began working as a session-man. In 1962 Robert Wyatt and others formed the Wilde Flowers, the beginning…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":35,"text":"…MacLise: The Invasion Of Thunderbolt Pagoda (1968) • Mad River: self-titled (Capitol, 1968) • Mad River: Paradise Bar And Grill (Capitol, 1969) • John Mayall: Hard Road (Decca, 1967) • Meters: self-titled (Josie, 1969) • Steve Miller: Children Of The Future (Capitol, 1968) • Steve Miller: Sailor (Capitol,…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":45,"text":"…(Gaza Strip and West Bank) • 1967: Rudi Dutschke leads student riots in West Berlin 3.2 The age of the revivals 1966-69 Blues revival When John Mayall (3) released the album Bluesbreakers (1966), featuring the former Yardbirds guitarist Eric Clapton, he defined, once and for all, a genre of…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":46,"text":"…(formed by those cohorts) would be the logical consequences of Mayall's continuing experiment with the blues. In the USA, the equivalent of John Mayall was Al Kooper, the keyboardist who had invented the sound of Bob Dylan's Like A Rolling Stone and Blonde On Blonde, i.e. of rock music as we know…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":46,"text":"…revival. Continuing to parallel Mayall's career, Kooper later gave the pop-jazz movement one of its most successful bands, the Blood Sweat & Tears. Mayall and Kooper inspired countless groups on both sides of the Atlantic. Case in point, three seasoned British blues musicians (Jack Bruce, Eric Clapton…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":60,"text":"Two line-ups that worked for John Mayall spawned two of the most creative bands of early prog-rock. Colosseum (10) recorded Valentyne Suite (1969), whose title-track was a side-long…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":314,"text":"…have four number-one hits and the Four Tops have two, all of them written by Tamla's team of Brian Holland, Lamond Dozier and Eddie Holland ？ John Mayall's Bluesbreakers, featuring Eric Clapton, import Chicago's rhythm and blues to Britain and become the epitome of \"blues-rock\" ？ Country Joe McDonald…"}],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Manchester Origin","body":"John Mayall is included in a list of numerous artists with roots in Manchester. \"John Mayl.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Oral History of \"Madchester\" - Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=q_Scyr2czh8"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Manchester Origins","body":"John Mayall is listed among the numerous bands from Manchester, emphasizing the city's prolific musical output. \"loads of loads of Aces, Buzcocs of Fall, James, Godly and Cream, Badly Drone Boy, Elbow, you know, John Mayl, Star Sailor-Verb, Phil Linnett, group of Manchester, Smith and you are a simply red, all his, you know, he goes on and on and this, this is bands and bands and bands, you know.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"The Ongoing History of New Music","episode":"The Oral History of Manchester Part 2","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KF_adyXf86Q"}]}],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Award for Best Contemporary Blues Album (1994) — Wake Up Call","body":"Nominated.","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3492779"}]},{"kind":"award","title